On Android 11 (API 30), system certificates are stored directly in /system/etc/security/cacerts/, so the standard adb remount method works cleanly without needing any Android 14+ workarounds.
Make sure your API 30 virtual device is a Google APIs image (not Google Play) and that your Burp Suite certificate (cacert.der) is saved in your current directory.

Complete Setup Commands for Android 11 (API 30)


1. Launch Emulator & Unlock System Partition

Run these commands in your Ubuntu terminal after closing any open emulator windows:
bash
# List your AVDs to get the exact name
~/Android/Sdk/emulator/emulator -list-avds

# Launch your API 30 emulator with writable system access (replace <AVD_NAME>)
~/Android/Sdk/emulator/emulator -avd <AVD_NAME> -writable-system &

# Wait for the emulator to boot completely to the home screen, then run:
adb root
adb shell avbctl disable-verification
adb reboot

# Wait for the reboot to finish, then remount as read-write
adb wait-for-device
adb root
adb remount
Use code with caution.

2. Format & Push the PortSwigger Certificate

Once adb remount prints remount succeeded, run these commands to install the certificate:
bash
# Convert DER to PEM and rename using the old subject hash
openssl x509 -inform DER -in cacert.der -out cacert.pem
HASH=$(openssl x509 -inform PEM -subject_hash_old -in cacert.pem | head -1)
mv cacert.pem ${HASH}.0

# Push to the Android 11 system store and set proper permissions
adb push ${HASH}.0 /system/etc/security/cacerts/
adb shell chmod 644 /system/etc/security/cacerts/${HASH}.0

# Reboot to load the certificate
adb reboot
Use code with caution.

Verify Installation

After the final reboot, open Settings > Security > Encryption & credentials > Trusted credentials on your emulator. Under the System tab, scroll down to PortSwigger to confirm the toggle is enabled.
